{0 CamlCast}

A first-person raycasting engine in OCaml on top of SDL2 ([tsdl]).

A {!Camlcast_core.World} is a graph of independently-authored {!Camlcast_core.Room}s, each
in its own coordinates, joined at named doorways that you both see and walk
through: an open one is a portal, drawn recursively into the opening, and a
{!Camlcast_core.Transform} carries the camera and the ray across. Rooms are built
from wall {e segments} at any angle, not cells of a grid; the floor is an
inclined {!Camlcast_core.Plane} and the ceiling — per room — is an inclined plane or
open {!Camlcast_core.Sky}; walls carry their own heights and {!Camlcast_core.Material}s,
can be see-through, and can be hung with decals; {!Camlcast_core.Image} sprites stand
in the world facing the player; and there is mouse look with pitch. Each frame is
drawn into a CPU pixel buffer: the floor, ceiling and sky are cast per pixel, the
opaque walls (with their decals) are painted over them, and then the sprites and
the see-through walls together, farthest first, so that a sprite can stand in
front of a window and be seen through one behind it. Surfaces take their
patterns from a procedural {!Camlcast_core.Texture} and their colour from their
material, dimmed by the world's {!Camlcast_core.Atmosphere}. The buffer is then
scaled to the window by the GPU.

{1 Three guides}

- {{!page-"making-a-game"} {b Making a game on CamlCast}} — from an empty
  directory to a game, one feature at a time, with the demo that isolates
  each one. Start here if you want to build something on the engine.
- {{!page-"building-the-engine"} {b Building the engine from scratch}} — how the
  picture is drawn, rebuilt by hand in eight phases with every derivation written
  out. Start here if you want to know how a raycaster works.
- {{!page-"building-the-layer"} {b Building the layer from scratch}} — how a
  description of a world becomes one, and how a component keeps state across a
  frame that rebuilt everything: elements, reconciling, hooks on effect
  handlers, and the seam between the two. The second half of the one above.

{1 The libraries}

The engine holds no content: not one colour, pattern, picture or room. What it
has instead are the types those things are values of, so a game supplies its own
and two games can share an engine without sharing a look.

- {b [camlcast]} — {b what a game opens.} The parts a world is described with,
  the components that hold its state, and the loop that plays one. See
  {!Camlcast}.
- {b [camlcast.loom]} — the runtime under that: elements, reconciling, hooks,
  contexts, a store. It depends on nothing but the standard library and knows
  nothing of walls.
- {b [camlcast.core]} — the platform. Geometry, ray casting, rendering, SDL. A
  game that genuinely needs {!Camlcast_core.World} or
  {!Camlcast_core.Renderer} adds this to its dune file and says so, which makes
  reaching past the layer a decision rather than a drift.
- {b [camlcast-demo]} — the demos and the art they are made of, run by
  [camlcast-demo]. One small world per engine feature, plus the five-room
  showcase that has all of them at once. It is content, and it lives outside the
  library it is content for; it stays here because between them the demos
  exercise every corner of the engine.

{1 Building a game on it}

A game hands over what its world should be, every frame, and never touches the
loop. {!Camlcast.Run.play} opens a window, plays a description on it until the
player quits, and closes it again; {!Camlcast.Run.on} is the same run on a window
already open, which is what a launcher wants so that going from its menu to a
level is the picture changing rather than the window going away.

{[
  let () = ignore (Run.play ~title:"A game" level)
]}

A description is made with {!Camlcast.P} — [world], [room], [outline], [doorway],
[sprite], [hud] — and the parts of it that have to remember something are
{e components}: functions from props to a description, holding state with
{!Camlcast.Hook.use_state} and reaching the outside world through
{!Camlcast.Hook.use_effect}. {!Camlcast.Events} is how one reads the frame it is
being rendered for: how long the last one took, what the player is pressing, what
the crosshair is on, which doorways were crossed. {!Camlcast.Store} is for what
belongs to no component in particular — a score, an inventory, which chapter the
player is in.

Everything the loop acts on by itself is one record, {!Camlcast.Controls.t}:
which controls walk and look, which leaves the run, which works whatever the
crosshair is on, and which shows the map over the world. Each is a list of
{!Camlcast_core.Input.control}s, so a mouse button and a key do the same job and
an empty list turns something off.

It reports how the run ended, as a {!Camlcast.Run.ending}: [Closed] for a window
that was shut, [Left] for a run that finished on its own terms — a leaving
control, or a description that said {!Camlcast.P.finish}. Only something that
plays one run after another needs the difference.

{2 The platform underneath}

None of the above is where the loop actually is. {!Camlcast_core.Engine.run}
plays a state of whatever type a game likes, described by an
{!Camlcast_core.Engine.type-game}: [update], given the frame's length, the
movement asked for and what the player is holding; [view], which world and which
player to draw from; and optionally [overlay], [pointing], [finished] and
[bindings]. {!Camlcast_core.Engine.run_world} is that loop over a bare
{!Camlcast_core.World} and the player walking it, plus an optional
[extend : World.t -> Player.t -> World.t] called on any frame the player crossed
into another room on.

That is the API this engine had before it had a layer over the top, and it is
still the floor the layer stands on — {!Camlcast.Run} is one call to
{!Camlcast_core.Engine.run} with a mount kept beside it. A game reaches for it
when it genuinely needs a {!Camlcast_core.World} or a {!Camlcast_core.Renderer},
by adding [camlcast.core] to its dune file and saying so.

Input arrives as controls and not as actions — {!Camlcast_core.Input.pressed},
{!Camlcast_core.Input.released}, {!Camlcast_core.Input.val-down} and
{!Camlcast_core.Input.held_for} over a {!Camlcast_core.Key} or a mouse button. What
"interact" or "journal" mean is a game's own table from the one to the other.

Walking is that too. {!Camlcast_core.Binding} is the table a game hands over, and it
covers the four movement axes, the key that goes fullscreen and the key — if any
— that ends the run; {!Camlcast_core.Binding.default} is WASD, the arrows and the
mouse, and it is a default and not a rule. An axis adds up terms, and the two
kinds are added up differently: a held control or a stick is a {e rate}, summed,
clamped to [[-1, 1]] and paid out at the axis's speed over the frame, while the
mouse is a {e displacement} that has already moved and is added as it stands.
Reading a table is {!Camlcast_core.Binding.motion}, which touches nothing and is
where a second kind of device would arrive.

Time passes only while the window has focus.

{1 The house rules}

The API is written to a few conventions, so that knowing one module is knowing
them all.

- {b Constructors are [make].} Where a value has one natural way in, it is
  [Module.make], with configuration labelled and the subject of the call — a
  picture, a material, two endpoints — positional at the end. Domain-named
  constructors stand beside it where the domain has better words:
  {!Camlcast_core.Color.rgb}, {!Camlcast_core.Plane.horizontal},
  {!Camlcast_core.Texture.generate}, {!Camlcast_core.Transform.between},
  {!Camlcast_core.Room.doorway}.
- {b All-optional constructors end in [()]}, and each names its defaults in
  its own doc: {!Camlcast_core.Atmosphere.make}, {!Camlcast_core.Sky.make},
  {!Camlcast_core.Binding.make}, {!Camlcast_core.Engine.val-game}. Where the default is
  a value worth having, it is also exported — {!Camlcast_core.Atmosphere.default},
  {!Camlcast_core.Sky.default}, {!Camlcast_core.Binding.default} — as the thing to start
  from and perturb.
- {b Reading a file is [load]}, always [string -> (t, [ `Msg of string ])
  result], and every fallible call in the library carries that same closed
  error: {!Camlcast_core.Bitmap.load}, {!Camlcast_core.Image.load},
  {!Camlcast_core.Texture.load}, and the [of_asset] pair that goes through
  {!Camlcast_core.Asset} first.
- {b Every number states its unit and range where it is declared.} Distances
  and heights are in cells (one cell = one texture repeat; the eye stands
  {!Camlcast_core.Config.eye_height} up); angles are radians on
  {!Camlcast_core.Vec.of_angle}'s reckoning; colour channels and alpha run 0 .. 255;
  fades and fractions run [0. .. 1.]. What a constructor would divide by or
  fade across, it checks — refusals are written as the negation of what would
  pass, so a [nan] is refused with the out-of-range.
- {b Changing an immutable value}: [with_x] hands back the same thing with one
  component replaced ({!Camlcast_core.Room.with_sprites},
  {!Camlcast_core.World.with_atmosphere} — and {!Camlcast_core.Room.with_door}, whose
  "thing" is a single threshold); [add_x]
  appends ({!Camlcast_core.Room.add_decal}, {!Camlcast_core.World.add_room}); [set_x] is
  an engine-mediated change that keeps a cross-cutting invariant
  ({!Camlcast_core.World.set_door}, which changes both sides of a link at once).
- {b The winding rule is stated once}, at the top of {!Camlcast_core.Room}, and
  everything else points there.

{1 Where to start}

{!Camlcast_core.Ray} carries the ray-versus-segment derivation and why the distance
it reports is free of fish-eye. {!Camlcast_core.Plane} carries the one-line equation
that casts the sloped floor and ceiling per pixel. {!Camlcast_core.Viewport} ties
them to the screen: the projection, the eye height, the pitch shear, and — since
the window is resizable and can go fullscreen at any frame — the rules that
decide what a resize does. {!Camlcast_core.Renderer} puts it all together.
{!Camlcast_core.World} carries both the portal machinery and the three primitives a
world grows by.

{1 The engine}

Each depends only on the ones above it.

- {!Camlcast_core.Config} — every tunable constant, in one place
- {!Camlcast_core.Key} — the keyboard, named by the engine: a place on the board
  rather than a letter, so a binding survives a change of layout
- {!Camlcast_core.Result_ext} — [let*] and [let+], for chaining fallible SDL calls,
  and the acquire/use/release that frees a window however a frame ends
- {!Camlcast_core.Vec} — immutable 2-D vectors, with the dot and cross products the
  geometry needs
- {!Camlcast_core.Transform} — rigid motions between room-local coordinate frames
- {!Camlcast_core.Plane} — an inclined floor/ceiling plane and its casting equation
- {!Camlcast_core.Color} — 8-bit RGB, shading and blending
- {!Camlcast_core.Bitmap} — decoding a PNG or JPEG into plain bytes: the one place
  pixel formats appear
- {!Camlcast_core.Asset} — where a file is, searched relative to the executable
  rather than to a source tree
- {!Camlcast_core.Texture} — colour surface patterns, generated or loaded, and
  the wrapping value noise the generated ones are built from
- {!Camlcast_core.Material} — what a surface is made of: its pattern
- {!Camlcast_core.Door} — a leaf hung in a doorway: open or closed, and what of
- {!Camlcast_core.Atmosphere} — the air a world is seen through: its fog, its haze,
  and where its light comes from
- {!Camlcast_core.Sky} — the open sky where a room has no roof
- {!Camlcast_core.Image} — full-colour images with alpha, for decals and sprites
- {!Camlcast_core.Framebuffer} — a CPU pixel buffer and the streaming texture it
  uploads through
- {!Camlcast_core.Paint} — clipped rectangles, lines and pictures over a finished
  frame
- {!Camlcast_core.Font} — a bitmap font on a fixed grid: layout, wrapping and
  drawing
- {!Camlcast_core.Room} — one room's walls, thresholds, surfaces, sprites and
  collision
- {!Camlcast_core.World} — named rooms, linked portals, the world's air and spawn,
  and how a world grows
- {!Camlcast_core.Ray} — ray-versus-segment intersection: every wall the ray
  crosses, farthest first
- {!Camlcast_core.Player} — the camera, as position + unit [dir] + unit [right],
  plus the pitch it looks up and down by
- {!Camlcast_core.Viewport} — window size to camera geometry; the eye height, the
  pitch shear and the resize rules
- {!Camlcast_core.Sight} — what the crosshair is on, traced through doorways
- {!Camlcast_core.Input} — the keyboard and the mouse as they are: controls, their
  edges and holds, and what the analog sources read
- {!Camlcast_core.Binding} — what those controls are for: a game's table, and the one
  pure function that turns a frame of them into a movement
- {!Camlcast_core.Renderer} — the software renderer: casts the planes, paints the
  walls, per pixel
- {!Camlcast_core.Clock} — the pacing arithmetic the loop measures its frames by,
  apart from any window
- {!Camlcast_core.Engine} — window lifetime, fullscreen state, and the game loop

{1 The demos}

[camlcast-demo] with no argument opens the list in a window — [--list] prints
it instead — and [camlcast-demo <name>] runs one.
Each is a single file with the feature it demonstrates as the only thing in it.
They are the other package, so their pages are its own:
{{:../camlcast-demo/index.html} the demos}.
